<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!--
        Notification emails. Email clients strip <style> blocks and cannot load
        @font-face, so every value here is inline and the font is a fallback
        chain ending in a websafe face. These are the only places outside
        primary_variables.scss where a brand hex is written by hand; the values
        are the ones in brand.py (primary #1E3A8A, ink #0F172A, paper #F7F7F5,
        graphite #4B5563, hairline #E5E7EB).

        Where a change only adds properties, it is written with
        `<attribute name="style" add="..." separator=" "/>` so an upstream edit
        to a property we do not set survives; only the places that must
        *remove* an upstream value (Verdana, #F1F1F1, the "Powered by" line)
        restate the whole node.

        The CTA cell is deliberately untouched: it already reads
        company.email_secondary_color (fill) and company.email_primary_color
        (text), which afenda_brand sets to Ledger Blue on white.

        THE BRAND HEADER ROW, both layouts
        The mark used to sit in the footer, inside upstream's "Powered by"
        line. It now leads the layout as a letterhead carrying two marks:

          * the tenant's logo, left and 32px tall - the subject of the mail;
          * the AFENDA mark, right and 16px tall - the maker's mark.

        Hierarchy is order and size, not a "powered by" label.

        Everything is <table>/<tr>/<td>. Outlook renders HTML through Word,
        which lays out tables and not divs or flexbox; that is why this whole
        file is table-based and moving the logo does not change it.

        Tenant logo, four constraints that do not apply on the auth page:

        1. The `?company=` is not optional. /web/binary/company_logo takes the
           company from the query string when present and otherwise falls back
           to `request.session.uid or SUPERUSER_ID` and reads THAT user's
           company (addons/web/controllers/binary.py:264-287). A mail client
           fetches with no Odoo session, so the fallback would silently serve
           the superuser's company logo on a multi-company database. The URL is
           also absolute: an email has no origin.
        2. Most clients block remote images until the recipient opts in, so for
           a large share of recipients the alt text *is* the header. Both alts
           are therefore real names - the company's own, and ours - never
           upstream's generic "Logo".
        3. Outlook's Word engine ignores CSS sizing, so both images carry
           width=/height= HTML attributes as well as the inline px.
           The tenant image carries height only, on purpose: logo_web is
           `image_process(logo, size=(180, 0))`
           (odoo/addons/base/models/res_company.py:169-172) - at most 180 wide
           with a free height, so its aspect ratio is unknown at render time
           and any fixed width/height pair would distort it. Fixing the height
           and letting the width follow is the only non-distorting choice; it
           bounds the row's vertical rhythm, which is what a header needs.
        4. The empty-logo case cannot arise. `uses_default_logo` is
           `not company.logo or company.logo == default_logo`
           (res_company.py:179-182), so `not uses_default_logo` already means
           "this tenant uploaded artwork of their own" - which both keeps
           /web/binary/company_logo from ever falling through to the 180x79
           transparent web/static/img/nologo.png (binary.py:305) and keeps us
           from presenting AFENDA's own shipped logo as if it were a tenant's.

        Upstream: addons/mail/data/mail_templates_email_layouts.xml.
    -->

    <!-- Default notification layout (addons/mail/data/mail_templates_email_layouts.xml:4) -->
    <template id="mail_notification_layout" inherit_id="mail.mail_notification_layout"
              name="AFENDA mail notification layout">
        <!-- :9 - upstream ships Verdana on #454748; both have to go, so this
             one replaces rather than adds. -->
        <xpath expr="//body" position="attributes">
            <attribute name="style">font-family:'Source Sans 3','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; color:#0F172A;</attribute>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :18 - the first child of the 900px container (:17). Inserting
             before it puts the brand row above everything mail emits.
             Deliberately NOT gated on show_header: that flag turns the CTA and
             record-name block on and off, and the mark has to be there whether
             or not the message carries a button. logo_email_2x.png is a 128x32
             transparent mark drawn for this 64x16 box, not the 1200px lockup;
             displaying it any larger would upscale it. -->
        <xpath expr="//div[@itemtype='http://schema.org/EmailMessage']" position="before">
            <table role="presentation" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" border="0"
                   style="width:100%; border-collapse:separate;">
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td align="left" valign="middle" style="padding:0 8px 12px 0;">
                            <img t-if="company and not company.uses_default_logo"
                                 t-att-src="'%s/web/binary/company_logo?company=%s' % (company.get_base_url(), company.id)"
                                 height="32" t-att-alt="company.name"
                                 style="height:32px;width:auto;border:0;vertical-align:middle;"/>
                        </td>
                        <td align="right" valign="middle" style="padding:0 0 12px 8px;">
                            <img t-att-src="company.get_base_url() + '/afenda_brand/static/img/logo_email_2x.png'"
                                 width="64" height="16" alt="AFENDA xForge"
                                 style="width:64px;height:16px;max-width:64px;border:0;vertical-align:middle;"/>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :85-91 - the "Powered by" line. OCA mail_debranding only strips
             anchors whose href matches odoo.com (mail_debranding/models/
             mail_render_mixin.py:28-35); the rebrand rewrote this href to the
             brand domain, so the anchor survives rendering and is ours to
             remove. The whole div is restated rather than just the anchor
             because "Powered by " is a text node of the div and no xpath
             position can reach it - replacing only the <a> would leave the
             words dangling in front of whatever took its place.

             What the footer keeps: the tagline, as plain text, not a link.
             The mark now identifies the sender at the top, so repeating it
             here would be duplication; but the cell cannot go empty either,
             because upstream's unfollow span opens with a "|" separator that
             would be orphaned. A quiet sign-off in graphite is the one thing
             that both reads correctly before that separator and leaves nothing
             outbound. The span itself is verbatim upstream: mail_mail strips
             it with a regex keyed on `id="mail_unfollow"`
             (addons/mail/models/mail_mail.py:23,383). Its link colour moves
             from Odoo's #555555 to graphite, since the block is restated
             anyway and #555555 is not a value brand.py declares. -->
        <xpath expr="//div[a[contains(@href, 'utm_source=db')]]" position="replace">
            <div t-if="show_footer" style="color: #4B5563; font-size:11px;">
                The truth of your business, kept.
                <span t-if="show_unfollow" id="mail_unfollow">
                    | <a href="/mail/unfollow" style="text-decoration:none; color:#4B5563;">Unfollow</a>
                </span>
            </div>
        </xpath>
    </template>

    <!-- Light notification layout (addons/mail/data/mail_templates_email_layouts.xml:96) -->
    <template id="mail_notification_light" inherit_id="mail.mail_notification_light"
              name="AFENDA light mail notification layout">
        <!-- :101 - upstream leaves <body> unstyled and puts the face on the
             outer table; set it on both so a client that drops table styles
             still gets the stack. -->
        <xpath expr="//body" position="attributes">
            <attribute name="style">font-family:'Source Sans 3','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; color:#0F172A;</attribute>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :104 - the frame around the card. Odoo grey #F1F1F1 and Verdana
             both have to go, so this one is a full rewrite. -->
        <xpath expr="//table[contains(@style, '#F1F1F1')]" position="attributes">
            <attribute name="style">padding-top: 16px; background-color: #F7F7F5; font-family:'Source Sans 3','Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif; color:#0F172A; width: 100%; border-collapse:separate;</attribute>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :105 - the card itself: white on paper needs a hairline to read.
             Appended, so the later `color` wins over upstream's #454748 and
             any property upstream adds later is kept. -->
        <xpath expr="//table[@width='590']" position="attributes">
            <attribute name="style" add="color:#0F172A; border:1px solid #E5E7EB;" separator=" "/>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :126-128 - upstream already draws the tenant logo, 48px tall, to
             the RIGHT of the record name. It moves into the brand row below,
             so this cell has to go: leaving it would render the tenant's
             artwork twice and put our mark ahead of theirs, which is the
             opposite of the intended order. The row then has one cell and the
             rule beneath it still spans colspan="2", which is harmless. -->
        <xpath expr="//td[img[contains(@t-att-src, '/logo.png?company')]]" position="replace"/>
        <!-- :107-108 - the brand row, first <tr> of the card's own <tbody>,
             above upstream's HEADER row. No rule under it: the record block
             below already ends in an <hr> (:130) and a second line 40px above
             it would read as noise. -->
        <xpath expr="//table[@width='590']/tbody/tr[1]" position="before">
            <tr>
                <td align="center" style="min-width: 590px; padding: 0 0 16px 0;">
                    <table role="presentation" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%"
                           style="padding: 0; border-collapse:separate;">
                        <tr>
                            <td align="left" valign="middle" style="padding:0 8px 0 0;">
                                <img t-if="company and not company.uses_default_logo"
                                     t-att-src="'%s/web/binary/company_logo?company=%s' % (company.get_base_url(), company.id)"
                                     height="32" t-att-alt="company.name"
                                     style="height:32px;width:auto;border:0;vertical-align:middle;"/>
                            </td>
                            <td align="right" valign="middle" style="padding:0 0 0 8px;">
                                <img t-att-src="company.get_base_url() + '/afenda_brand/static/img/logo_email_2x.png'"
                                     width="64" height="16" alt="AFENDA xForge"
                                     style="width:64px;height:16px;max-width:64px;border:0;vertical-align:middle;"/>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </table>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :143 - the company block's footer cell; padding-top after
             upstream's shorthand. Anchored on upstream's own padding value
             rather than on `font-size:11px`, which the replaced "Powered by"
             cell below now also carries. -->
        <xpath expr="//td[contains(@style, 'padding: 0 8px 0 8px')]" position="attributes">
            <attribute name="style" add="border-top:1px solid #E5E7EB; padding-top:12px; color:#4B5563;" separator=" "/>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :144 - that cell opens with its own grey rule, which would now draw
             a second line directly under the hairline border above. Collapse it
             rather than delete it, so the cell keeps upstream's structure. -->
        <xpath expr="//td[contains(@style, 'padding: 0 8px 0 8px')]/hr" position="attributes">
            <attribute name="style">border:medium none;clear:both;display:block;font-size:0px;height:0;line-height:0;margin:0;</attribute>
        </xpath>
        <!-- :159-165 - the "Powered by" cell, for the reasons given on the
             default layout above. This row sits on the paper frame, outside
             the white card. -->
        <xpath expr="//td[a[contains(@href, 'utm_source=db')]]" position="replace">
            <td align="center" style="min-width: 590px; padding: 8px 0 0 0; color:#4B5563; font-size:11px;">
                The truth of your business, kept.
                <span t-if="show_unfollow" id="mail_unfollow">
                    | <a href="/mail/unfollow" style="text-decoration:none; color:#4B5563;">Unfollow</a>
                </span>
            </td>
        </xpath>
    </template>

</odoo>
